PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L09U2.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":9,"cefr":"B2b","unit":2,"theme":"Innovation and Enterprise","essential_question":"What does it take to turn an idea into a successful business?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1","title":"If I Hadn't Seen the Problem, I Wouldn't Be Here: Mixed Conditionals","objectives":["Students will be able to use mixed conditionals to connect past events with present results and present situations with past results.","Students will be able to choose between second, third and mixed conditionals according to time.","I can say \"If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company now.\"","I can say \"If I were more afraid of failure, I would have given up.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام الجمل الشرطية المختلطة لربط أحداث الماضي بنتائج حاضرة، وأوضاع الحاضر بنتائج ماضية.","سيتمكن الطلاب من الاختيار بين الشرط الثاني والثالث والمختلط بحسب الزمن.","أستطيع أن أقول If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company now.","أستطيع أن أقول If I were more afraid of failure, I would have given up."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"startup","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈstɑrˌtʌp/","definition":"a new, small company, often based on a new idea or technology","arabic":"شركة ناشئة","example":"Riyadh has hundreds of technology startups.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-W01"},{"word":"founder","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfaʊndɚ/","definition":"a person who starts a company or organization","arabic":"مؤسِّس","example":"The founder of the company was only twenty-two.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-W02"},{"word":"investor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈvɛstɚ/","definition":"a person who gives money to a business hoping to make a profit","arabic":"مستثمر","example":"The investors were impressed by her business plan.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-W03"},{"word":"funding","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfʌndɪŋ/","definition":"money given for a particular purpose","arabic":"تمويل","example":"The startup received funding from a government program.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Mixed conditionals mix the times of the second and third conditionals. Type 1: a past condition with a present result. Form: If + past perfect, would / could / might + base verb: If I hadn't seen my neighbors' frustration (past), I wouldn't be running this company today (now).\nType 2: a present or general condition (something always true about a person) with a past result. Form: If + past simple, would / could / might have + past participle: If I were more afraid of failure (I'm not – a general truth), I would have given up (in the past).\nTo choose the right form, ask two questions: When is the condition? and When is the result? Second conditional = present + present (If I had money, I would invest). Third conditional = past + past (If I had had money, I would have invested). Mixed = the times are different. Use were for all subjects in formal English.\npast condition → present result: If + had done, would do / would be doing\npresent condition → past result: If + did / were, would have done\n2nd: present + present • 3rd: past + past\nsignal words: now, today, still (present result) • yesterday, last year (past)","examples":["If she hadn't won the competition, she wouldn't have investors today.","If I spoke Chinese, I would have applied for that job in Shanghai.","If they had launched the app earlier, they would be the market leader now.","If he were more careful, he wouldn't have lost his laptop.","If we hadn't moved to Riyadh, I wouldn't be studying at this school.","If the company weren't so innovative, it wouldn't have survived the crisis.","If I had taken that internship, I might be working in Dubai now.","Where would you live now if you had been born in another country?"],"common_errors":["✗ If I didn't see the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today. (the problem was in the past) → ✓ If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today. (Past condition → past perfect.)","✗ If he had studied medicine, he would have been a doctor now. → ✓ If he had studied medicine, he would be a doctor now. (Present result → would + base verb.)","✗ If she was braver, she would apply last year. → ✓ If she were braver, she would have applied last year. (Past result → would have + past participle.)","✗ If I would have saved money, I would have a car now. → ✓ If I had saved money, I would have a car now. (No would in the if-clause.)"],"arabic":"الشرط المختلط يمزج أزمنة الشرط الثاني والثالث.\nالنوع الأول: شرط في الماضي ونتيجة في الحاضر: If + الماضي التام، would + المصدر: If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today.\nالنوع الثاني: شرط في الحاضر (صفة عامة) ونتيجة في الماضي: If + الماضي البسيط، would have + التصريف الثالث: If I were more afraid of failure, I would have given up.\nلتختار الصيغة اسأل: متى الشرط؟ ومتى النتيجة؟"},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “If I Hadn't Seen the Problem, I Wouldn't Be Here: Mixed Conditionals”. Let’s learn mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U2-LS1 | If I Hadn't Seen the Problem, I Wouldn't Be Here: Mixed Conditionals","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “From Kitchen to Company”: Six years ago, Reem was a computer science student in Jeddah who loved her grandmother's cooking. Today, she runs Sufra, an app that connects home cooks with customers across the Kingdom. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Six years ago, Reem was a computer science student in Jeddah who loved her grandmother's cooking. Today, she runs Sufra, an app that connects home cooks with customers across the Kingdom.","visual":"Image GV-L09-U2-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Mixed conditionals mix the times of the second and third conditionals. Type 1: a past condition with a present result. Form: If + past perfect, would / could / might + base verb: If I hadn't seen my neighbors' frustration (past), I wouldn't be running this company today (now). Type 2: a present or general condition (something always true about a person) with a past result. Form: If + past simple, would / could / might have + past participle: If I were more afraid of failure (I'm not – a general truth), I would have given up (in the past).","on_screen":"past condition → present result: If + had done, would do / would be doing • present condition → past result: If + did / were, would have done • 2nd: present + present • 3rd: past + past","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. If she hadn't won the competition, she wouldn't have investors today. If I spoke Chinese, I would have applied for that job in Shanghai. If they had launched the app earlier, they would be the market leader now.","on_screen":"If she hadn't won the competition, she wouldn't have investors today. / If I spoke Chinese, I would have applied for that job in Shanghai. / If they had launched the app earlier, they would be the market leader now.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “If I didn't see the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today. (the problem was in the past).” That’s not right. We say: “If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today.” Past condition → past perfect.","on_screen":"✗ If I didn't see the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today. (the problem was in the past)   ✓ If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today.\" Did she see the problem? Is she running the company now? … Think! … Yes, she saw it. Yes, she runs it now. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company now.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can say \"If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company now.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الشرط المختلط يمزج أزمنة الشرط الثاني والثالث.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: If I didn't see the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today. (the problem was in the past) — الصحيح: If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقول If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company now."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"From Kitchen to Company","body":"Six years ago, Reem was a computer science student in Jeddah who loved her grandmother's cooking. Today, she runs Sufra, an app that connects home cooks with customers across the Kingdom.\nThe idea came by accident. During her final year, Reem noticed that many talented women in her neighborhood cooked delicious meals but had no way to sell them. \"If I hadn't seen my neighbors' frustration, I wouldn't be running this company today,\" she says.\nStarting a business was not easy. Reem had no money, and her first two apps failed. \"If I were more afraid of failure, I would have given up after the first mistake,\" she explains.\nIn 2021, Reem won a startup competition, and investors offered her funding. If she hadn't entered that competition, Sufra might still be a small project on her laptop. Now the company employs eighty people and works with more than three thousand home cooks.\nReem believes that young Saudis have more opportunities than ever. \"If young people weren't so creative, the startup scene wouldn't have grown so quickly in the last few years,\" she says.\nHer advice to students is simple: \"Solve a real problem. If you understand your customers, you'll build something they need.\"","word_count":203,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"If Reem hadn't seen her neighbors' frustration, she ___ this company today.","options":["won't run","wouldn't have run","hadn't run","wouldn't be running"],"answer":"wouldn't be running","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"If Reem ___ more afraid of failure, she would have given up after her first mistake.","options":["will be","is","were","had been being"],"answer":"were","explanation":"Present condition → past result.","explanation_ar":"شرط حاضر ← نتيجة ماضية."},{"q":"If I had taken that internship, I ___ in Dubai now.","options":["might have worked","will work","might be working","had worked"],"answer":"might be working","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"If I had enough money, I ___ in a startup.","options":["would invest","had invested","would have invested","will invest"],"answer":"would invest","explanation":"Second conditional (present + present).","explanation_ar":"الشرط الثاني."},{"q":"If they had launched the app earlier, they ___ the market leader last year.","options":["will be","would have been","would be","had been"],"answer":"would have been","explanation":"Third conditional (past + past).","explanation_ar":"الشرط الثالث."},{"q":"\"If young people weren't so creative, the startup scene ______ so quickly in recent years,\" the investor explained.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["wouldn't have grown","didn't grow","wouldn't grow","hadn't grown"],"answer":"wouldn't have grown","explanation":"Present condition → past result.","explanation_ar":"شرط حاضر ← نتيجة ماضية."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with the correct form. Word bank: had accepted, were, wouldn't be studying","items":[{"q":"If we hadn't moved to Riyadh, I ______ (not / study) at this school now.","options":[],"answer":"wouldn't be studying","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"If Adam ______ (be) more organized, he wouldn't have missed the deadline.","options":[],"answer":"were","explanation":"Present condition → past result.","explanation_ar":"شرط حاضر ← نتيجة ماضية."},{"q":"If she ______ (accept) the job offer, she would be living in London now.","options":[],"answer":"had accepted","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Write mixed conditional sentences about the real situations.","items":[{"q":"I didn't study design, so I'm not a designer now.  (mixed)","options":[],"answer":"If I had studied design, I would be a designer now.","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"He isn't patient, so he didn't wait for the results.  (mixed)","options":[],"answer":"If he were patient, he would have waited for the results.","explanation":"Present condition → past result.","explanation_ar":"شرط حاضر ← نتيجة ماضية."},{"q":"They didn't save money, so they can't buy a house now.  (mixed)","options":[],"answer":"If they had saved money, they could buy a house now.","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Imagine different paths.","items":[{"q":"How would your life be different now if you had been born in another country?","options":[],"answer":"If I had been born in Japan, I would speak Japanese now.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about a past decision that affects your life today.","options":[],"answer":"If I hadn't joined the robotics club, I wouldn't be so interested in engineering now.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about a personal quality that changed a past event.","options":[],"answer":"If I weren't so shy, I would have spoken at the school assembly.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a second conditional sentence about a business idea.","options":[],"answer":"If I had enough money, I would open a café for students.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a third conditional sentence about a missed chance.","options":[],"answer":"If I had entered the competition, I might have won.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Interview an entrepreneur.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 questions using mixed conditionals for an entrepreneur.","options":[],"answer":"Where would you be now if you hadn't started your company? If you weren't so determined, would you have succeeded?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 possible answers using mixed conditionals.","options":[],"answer":"If I hadn't started my company, I would probably be working in a bank. If I weren't so determined, I would have given up years ago.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence comparing a third conditional and a mixed conditional.","options":[],"answer":"If I had invested in that startup, I would have made money last year, and I would be rich now.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Four Corners Grammar","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["The teacher says a sentence frame with a cue word and four possible answers (A–D).","Students walk to the corner with the correct answer.","One student in each corner explains the choice in one sentence.","Correct corner scores a point; continue with the next cue."],"data":[{"label":"If I had…, I would be…"},{"label":"If I were…, I would have…"},{"label":"unless"},{"label":"as long as"},{"label":"provided that"},{"label":"otherwise"},{"label":"Suppose…"},{"label":"in case"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"If he had studied medicine, he ___ a doctor now.","options":["had been","will be","would have been","would be"],"answer":"would be","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"If I ___ so busy, I would have come to your party last week.","options":["weren't","hadn't been being","won't be","am not"],"answer":"weren't","explanation":"Present condition → past result.","explanation_ar":"شرط حاضر ← نتيجة ماضية."},{"q":"If the company had hired better engineers, the product ___ so many problems now.","options":["wouldn't have had","won't have","wouldn't have","hadn't had"],"answer":"wouldn't have","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"","options":["If she would speak French, she would have gotten the job.","If she spoke French, she would get the job in Paris last year.","If she spoke French, she would have gotten the job in Paris.","If she had spoken French, she would get the job last year."],"answer":"If she spoke French, she would have gotten the job in Paris.","explanation":"Present condition → past result.","explanation_ar":"شرط حاضر ← نتيجة ماضية."},{"q":"If you ___ the instructions, you wouldn't be lost now.","options":["have read","had read","would read","read"],"answer":"had read","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"If our school had a bigger budget, we ___ a new lab.","options":["will build","had built","would have built","would build"],"answer":"would build","explanation":"Second conditional (present + present).","explanation_ar":"الشرط الثاني."},{"q":"\"If I hadn't met my business partner, I wouldn't be here today.\" Did the speaker meet a business partner?","options":["No.","Yes.","We can't tell.","Not yet."],"answer":"Yes.","explanation":"What really happened?","explanation_ar":"ماذا حدث فعلًا؟"},{"q":"If the founders hadn't patented their invention in 2015, their competitors ______ similar products today.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["could be selling","could have sold","had sold","can sell"],"answer":"could be selling","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"If the engineer weren't so careful with every detail, she ______ the design flaw that saved the project last year.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["won't notice","wouldn't have noticed","wouldn't notice","didn't notice"],"answer":"wouldn't have noticed","explanation":"Present condition → past result.","explanation_ar":"شرط حاضر ← نتيجة ماضية."},{"q":"Which sentence is a mixed conditional?","options":["If I learned to code, I would build an app.","If I learn to code, I will build an app.","If I had learned to code, I would have built an app.","If I had learned to code, I would have my own app now."],"answer":"If I had learned to code, I would have my own app now.","explanation":"What really happened?","explanation_ar":"ماذا حدث فعلًا؟"}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A young Saudi founder in a bright startup office in Jeddah, standing in front of a screen showing a food-delivery app called Sufra; a smaller picture shows her as a student in a home kitchen with her grandmother; investors clapping at a startup competition. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A young Saudi founder in a bright startup office in Jeddah, standing in front of a screen showing a food-delivery app called Sufra; a smaller picture shows her as a student in a home kitchen with her grandmother; investors clapping at a startup competition"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb)"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows startup (a new, small company, often based on a new idea or technology); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing startup: a new, small company, often based on a new idea or technology"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows founder (a person who starts a company or organization); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing founder: a person who starts a company or organization"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows investor (a person who gives money to a business hoping to make a profit); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing investor: a person who gives money to a business hoping to make a profit"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows funding (money given for a particular purpose); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing funding: money given for a particular purpose"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Four Corners Grammar”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Four Corners Grammar"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-W01","script":"startup. startup. Riyadh has hundreds of technology startups.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-W02","script":"founder. founder. The founder of the company was only twenty-two.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-W03","script":"investor. investor. The investors were impressed by her business plan.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-W04","script":"funding. funding. The startup received funding from a government program.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-T1","script":"Six years ago, Reem was a computer science student in Jeddah who loved her grandmother's cooking. Today, she runs Sufra, an app that connects home cooks with customers across the Kingdom. The idea came by accident. During her final year, Reem noticed that many talented women in her neighborhood cooked delicious meals but had no way to sell them. \"If I hadn't seen my neighbors' frustration, I wouldn't be running this company today,\" she says. Starting a business was not easy. Reem had no money, and her first two apps failed. \"If I were more afraid of failure, I would have given up after the first mistake,\" she explains. In 2021, Reem won a startup competition, and investors offered her funding. If she hadn't entered that competition, Sufra might still be a small project on her laptop. Now the company employs eighty people and works with more than three thousand home cooks. Reem believes that young Saudis have more opportunities than ever. \"If young people weren't so creative, the startup scene wouldn't have grown so quickly in the last few years,\" she says. Her advice to students is simple: \"Solve a real problem. If you understand your customers, you'll build something they need.\"","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “If I Hadn't Seen the Problem, I Wouldn't Be Here: Mixed Conditionals”. Let’s learn mixed conditionals: past condition → present result (If + past perfect, would + base verb); present/general condition → past result (If + past simple, would have + past participle); contrast with second and third conditionals together. Listen to the start of “From Kitchen to Company”: Six years ago, Reem was a computer science student in Jeddah who loved her grandmother's cooking. Today, she runs Sufra, an app that connects home cooks with customers across the Kingdom. Look at the green words. Mixed conditionals mix the times of the second and third conditionals. Type 1: a past condition with a present result. Form: If + past perfect, would / could / might + base verb: If I hadn't seen my neighbors' frustration (past), I wouldn't be running this company today (now). Type 2: a present or general condition (something always true about a person) with a past result. Form: If + past simple, would / could / might have + past participle: If I were more afraid of failure (I'm not – a general truth), I would have given up (in the past). Here are some examples. If she hadn't won the competition, she wouldn't have investors today. If I spoke Chinese, I would have applied for that job in Shanghai. If they had launched the app earlier, they would be the market leader now. Be careful! Some learners say: “If I didn't see the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today. (the problem was in the past).” That’s not right. We say: “If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today.” Past condition → past perfect. Quick check: \"If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company today.\" Did she see the problem? Is she running the company now? … Think! … Yes, she saw it. Yes, she runs it now. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"If I hadn't seen the problem, I wouldn't be running this company now.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2","title":"Provided That We Raise the Money…: Alternatives to if","objectives":["Students will be able to express conditions with unless, provided that, as long as, otherwise, suppose and even if.","Students will be able to distinguish in case (precaution) from if (condition).","I can say \"Provided that we raise the money, we'll launch next year.\"","I can explain the difference between \"in case it rains\" and \"if it rains\"."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التعبير عن الشروط بـ unless وprovided that وas long as وotherwise وsuppose وeven if.","سيتمكن الطلاب من التمييز بين in case (احتياط) وif (شرط).","أستطيع أن أقول Provided that we raise the money, we'll launch next year.","أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين in case it rains وif it rains."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"innovation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnəˈveɪʃən/","definition":"a new idea, method or product","arabic":"ابتكار","example":"Innovation is the key to success in technology.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS2-W01"},{"word":"prototype","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈproʊtəˌtaɪp/","definition":"the first model of a new product, used for testing","arabic":"نموذج أولي","example":"The team built a prototype of the robot in two weeks.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS2-W02"},{"word":"revenue","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɛvəˌnu/","definition":"the total money a company receives from sales","arabic":"الإيرادات","example":"The company's revenue doubled last year.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS2-W03"},{"word":"profit","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈprɑfət/","definition":"money you earn after paying all your costs","arabic":"ربح","example":"The café made a profit in its second year.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"unless = if… not: Unless we cut costs, we'll go bankrupt. (= If we don't cut costs…) Don't use a negative after unless: (not: Unless we don't cut costs).\nas long as / provided (that) / providing (that) / on condition that = only if (a strong condition): Provided that the investors agree, we'll launch next month. You can borrow my laptop as long as you return it tomorrow. suppose / supposing / what if introduce an imaginary situation: Suppose you won a million riyals—what would you do? otherwise = if not (at the start of a new clause): Save your work; otherwise, you'll lose it.\nin case = as a precaution (because something might happen): Bring a backup laptop in case yours stops working. (Bring it anyway.) Compare: Bring it if yours stops working. (Only bring it then.) even if = it doesn't matter whether: Even if we lose, we'll learn a lot. Like if, these words take a present tense for the future: (not: provided that we will raise) → provided that we raise.\nunless = if… not (no double negative)\nas long as / provided that / on condition that = only if\nsuppose / what if = imagine • otherwise = if not\nin case = precaution • even if = it doesn't matter whether","examples":["Unless you have a clear plan, investors won't listen.","The bank will lend you money provided that you have a business plan.","You can use the lab as long as you clean it afterwards.","Suppose a competitor copied your idea—what would you do?","We need to patent our invention; otherwise, others could copy it.","Save a copy of your presentation in case the internet fails.","Even if the first product fails, we'll learn valuable lessons.","The investor agreed to help on condition that he received 20% of the company."],"common_errors":["✗ Unless we don't advertise, nobody will know about us. → ✓ Unless we advertise, nobody will know about us. (unless already means \"if not\".)","✗ Provided that we will get funding, we'll hire staff. → ✓ Provided that we get funding, we'll hire staff. (Present tense after provided that.)","✗ Take a charger if your battery dies. (as a precaution) → ✓ Take a charger in case your battery dies. (Precaution → in case.)","✗ Hurry up, unless you'll miss the bus. → ✓ Hurry up; otherwise, you'll miss the bus. (\"if not\" at the start of a new clause → otherwise.)"],"arabic":"unless تعني if… not، فلا نضع بعدها نفيًا: Unless we cut costs, we'll go bankrupt.\nas long as وprovided that وon condition that تعني «بشرط أن / فقط إذا».\nsuppose / what if لموقف متخيَّل، وotherwise تعني «وإلا»، وeven if تعني «حتى لو».\nin case تعني «احتياطًا لـ»: Bring a backup laptop in case yours stops working، وتختلف عن if."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Provided That We Raise the Money…: Alternatives to if”. Let’s learn unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U2-LS2 | Provided That We Raise the Money…: Alternatives to if","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: As long as you work hard, you'll succeed. Unless the prototype works, investors won't give us money. Suppose the app failed—what would you do?","on_screen":"As long as you work hard, you'll succeed. | Unless the prototype works, investors won't give us money. | Suppose the app failed—what would you do?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"unless = if… not: Unless we cut costs, we'll go bankrupt. (= If we don't cut costs…) Don't use a negative after unless: (not: Unless we don't cut costs). as long as / provided (that) / providing (that) / on condition that = only if (a strong condition): Provided that the investors agree, we'll launch next month. You can borrow my laptop as long as you return it tomorrow. suppose / supposing / what if introduce an imaginary situation: Suppose you won a million riyals—what would you do? otherwise = if not (at the start of a new clause): Save your work; otherwise, you'll lose it.","on_screen":"unless = if… not (no double negative) • as long as / provided that / on condition that = only if • suppose / what if = imagine • otherwise = if not","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Unless you have a clear plan, investors won't listen. The bank will lend you money provided that you have a business plan. You can use the lab as long as you clean it afterwards.","on_screen":"Unless you have a clear plan, investors won't listen. / The bank will lend you money provided that you have a business plan. / You can use the lab as long as you clean it afterwards.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Unless we don't advertise, nobody will know about us.” That’s not right. We say: “Unless we advertise, nobody will know about us.” unless already means \"if not\".","on_screen":"✗ Unless we don't advertise, nobody will know about us.   ✓ Unless we advertise, nobody will know about us.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Unless we cut costs, we'll go bankrupt.\" What happens if we don't cut costs? … Think! … We'll go bankrupt. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"Provided that we raise the money, we'll launch next year.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can say \"Provided that we raise the money, we'll launch next year.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","unless تعني if… not، فلا نضع بعدها نفيًا: Unless we cut costs, we'll go bankrupt.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Unless we don't advertise, nobody will know about us. — الصحيح: Unless we advertise, nobody will know about us.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقول Provided that we raise the money, we'll launch next year."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ we cut costs, the company will go bankrupt.","options":["Provided","In case","As long as","Unless"],"answer":"Unless","explanation":"unless = if… not.","explanation_ar":"unless."},{"q":"The bank will lend you money ___ you have a clear business plan.","options":["unless","provided that","in case","otherwise"],"answer":"provided that","explanation":"as long as / provided that = only if.","explanation_ar":"بشرط أن."},{"q":"Save a copy of your presentation ___ the internet stops working.","options":["provided that","unless","even","in case"],"answer":"in case","explanation":"in case = as a precaution.","explanation_ar":"احتياطًا."},{"q":"We must patent our invention; ___, a competitor could copy it.","options":["otherwise","unless","in case","provided"],"answer":"otherwise","explanation":"otherwise = if not.","explanation_ar":"وإلا."},{"q":"___ the first version fails, we'll learn valuable lessons from it.","options":["Provided that","In case of","Even if","Unless"],"answer":"Even if","explanation":"even if = it doesn't matter whether.","explanation_ar":"حتى لو."},{"q":"Investors usually agree to fund a startup only ______ its founders can show that there is a real demand for their product.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["unless","in case","if","otherwise"],"answer":"if","explanation":"as long as / provided that = only if.","explanation_ar":"بشرط أن."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with unless, as long as, in case, otherwise or suppose. Word bank: otherwise, Suppose, as long as","items":[{"q":"You can borrow my laptop ______ you return it by Thursday.","options":[],"answer":"as long as","explanation":"as long as / provided that = only if.","explanation_ar":"بشرط أن."},{"q":"______ a big company offered to buy your startup—would you sell it?","options":[],"answer":"Suppose","explanation":"suppose / what if = imagine.","explanation_ar":"لنفترض."},{"q":"Write down your ideas immediately; ______, you might forget them.","options":[],"answer":"otherwise","explanation":"otherwise = if not.","explanation_ar":"وإلا."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Unless we don't advertise, nobody will know about our product.","options":[],"answer":"Unless we advertise, nobody will know about our product.","explanation":"Unless we don't advertise → Unless we advertise. unless = if… not.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Unless we don't advertise → Unless we advertise. unless."},{"q":"Provided that we will get funding, we'll hire five engineers.","options":[],"answer":"Provided that we get funding, we'll hire five engineers.","explanation":"will get → get. as long as / provided that = only if.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: will get → get. بشرط أن."},{"q":"Take some cash if the card machine doesn't work.","options":[],"answer":"Take some cash in case the card machine doesn't work.","explanation":"if → in case. in case = as a precaution.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: if → in case. احتياطًا."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Set your conditions.","items":[{"q":"Complete: \"I'll help you with your project as long as…\"","options":[],"answer":"I'll help you with your project as long as you share the work fairly.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Complete: \"Unless I study tonight, …\"","options":[],"answer":"Unless I study tonight, I won't pass tomorrow's quiz.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"in case\" about a trip.","options":[],"answer":"I'll pack a jacket in case it gets cold in Abha.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a \"Suppose…\" question about money or business.","options":[],"answer":"Suppose you could start any business—what would it be?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"even if\".","options":[],"answer":"Even if it rains, we'll go to the startup exhibition.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A partnership agreement.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 conditions for two friends starting a small business together.","options":[],"answer":"We'll share the profit equally as long as we both work the same hours. Neither of us can spend more than 500 riyals unless the other agrees. We'll continue provided that we make a profit within six months.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 precautions with \"in case\".","options":[],"answer":"We'll keep a spare printer in case the first one breaks. We'll save money every month in case sales drop.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"otherwise\".","options":[],"answer":"We must deliver orders on time; otherwise, customers will leave bad reviews.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Grammar Snakes & Ladders","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Roll and move. If you land on a cue square, make a sentence with the cue and unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether).","Correct at the bottom of a ladder: climb. Incorrect at the head of a snake: slide down.","Teammates are the judges and must explain any correction.","First player to reach square 30 wins."],"data":[{"cue":"I / not / study design (past) / not / be / an engineer (now)"},{"cue":"she / be / braver (now) / apply / last year"},{"cue":"we / not / win / the competition / not / have / funding now"},{"cue":"unless / we / cut costs / go bankrupt"},{"cue":"as long as / you / work hard / succeed"},{"cue":"provided that / the investors / agree / launch next month"},{"cue":"take an umbrella / in case / it / rain"},{"cue":"suppose / you / win / a million riyals"},{"cue":"save your work / otherwise / you / lose it"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"You won't succeed ___ you believe in your idea.","options":["in case","unless","provided","as long as"],"answer":"unless","explanation":"unless = if… not.","explanation_ar":"unless."},{"q":"The investor agreed to help ___ he received 20% of the company.","options":["in case","unless","otherwise","on condition that"],"answer":"on condition that","explanation":"as long as / provided that = only if.","explanation_ar":"بشرط أن."},{"q":"I always carry a power bank ___ my phone battery runs out.","options":["in case","provided","unless","as long as"],"answer":"in case","explanation":"in case = as a precaution.","explanation_ar":"احتياطًا."},{"q":"Check your spelling; ___, the judges will think you're careless.","options":["otherwise","even if","provided","unless"],"answer":"otherwise","explanation":"otherwise = if not.","explanation_ar":"وإلا."},{"q":"","options":["Even if we will lose the competition, we'll be proud of our work.","Even we lose the competition, we'll be proud of our work.","Even if we lose the competition, we'll be proud of our work.","Unless even we lose the competition, we'll be proud of our work."],"answer":"Even if we lose the competition, we'll be proud of our work.","explanation":"even if = it doesn't matter whether.","explanation_ar":"حتى لو."},{"q":"","options":["Supposed the app became popular, how would you handle the demand?","Supposing the app will become popular, how would you handle the demand?","Suppose if the app became popular, how would you handle the demand?","Supposing the app became popular, how would you handle the demand?"],"answer":"Supposing the app became popular, how would you handle the demand?","explanation":"suppose / what if = imagine.","explanation_ar":"لنفترض."},{"q":"","options":["You can join our team in case you're willing to learn.","You can join our team unless you're willing to learn.","You can join our team as long as you're willing to learn.","You can join our team as long as you'll be willing to learn."],"answer":"You can join our team as long as you're willing to learn.","explanation":"as long as / provided that = only if.","explanation_ar":"بشرط أن."},{"q":"The company will keep growing ______ it continues to invest in research; if it stops, competitors will quickly catch up.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["even though","as long as","unless","in case"],"answer":"as long as","explanation":"as long as / provided that = only if.","explanation_ar":"بشرط أن."},{"q":"Small businesses should keep emergency savings ______ sales suddenly fall during an economic crisis.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["as long as","in case","unless","provided that"],"answer":"in case","explanation":"in case = as a precaution.","explanation_ar":"احتياطًا."},{"q":"\"Unless we find an investor, we'll close the company.\" What will happen if they don't find an investor?","options":["They'll find an investor.","Nothing will change.","They'll open a new company.","They'll close the company."],"answer":"They'll close the company.","explanation":"unless = if… not.","explanation_ar":"unless."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A startup competition backstage: a team of students rehearsing their pitch with notes saying \"Unless we practice…\", \"Provided that the judges…\", \"Bring a backup laptop in case…\"; a mentor pointing at a checklist. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A startup competition backstage: a team of students rehearsing their pitch with notes saying \"Unless we practice…\", \"Provided that the judges…\", \"Bring a backup laptop in case…\"; a mentor pointing at a checklist"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether): a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Innovation and Enterprise”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of unless (= if… not)"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows innovation (a new idea, method or product); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing innovation: a new idea, method or product"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows prototype (the first model of a new product, used for testing); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing prototype: the first model of a new product, used for testing"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows revenue (the total money a company receives from sales); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing revenue: the total money a company receives from sales"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows profit (money you earn after paying all your costs); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing profit: money you earn after paying all your costs"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Grammar Snakes & Ladders”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Grammar Snakes & Ladders"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS2-W01","script":"innovation. innovation. Innovation is the key to success in technology.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS2-W02","script":"prototype. prototype. The team built a prototype of the robot in two weeks.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS2-W03","script":"revenue. revenue. The company's revenue doubled last year.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS2-W04","script":"profit. profit. The café made a profit in its second year.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Provided That We Raise the Money…: Alternatives to if”. Let’s learn unless (= if… not); as long as / provided (that) / providing / on condition that (= only if); suppose / supposing / what if (imaginary situations); otherwise (= if not); in case (precaution); even if (it doesn't matter whether) together. Remember these patterns: As long as you work hard, you'll succeed. Unless the prototype works, investors won't give us money. Suppose the app failed—what would you do? unless = if… not: Unless we cut costs, we'll go bankrupt. (= If we don't cut costs…) Don't use a negative after unless: (not: Unless we don't cut costs). as long as / provided (that) / providing (that) / on condition that = only if (a strong condition): Provided that the investors agree, we'll launch next month. You can borrow my laptop as long as you return it tomorrow. suppose / supposing / what if introduce an imaginary situation: Suppose you won a million riyals—what would you do? otherwise = if not (at the start of a new clause): Save your work; otherwise, you'll lose it. Here are some examples. Unless you have a clear plan, investors won't listen. The bank will lend you money provided that you have a business plan. You can use the lab as long as you clean it afterwards. Be careful! Some learners say: “Unless we don't advertise, nobody will know about us.” That’s not right. We say: “Unless we advertise, nobody will know about us.” unless already means \"if not\". Quick check: \"Unless we cut costs, we'll go bankrupt.\" What happens if we don't cut costs? … Think! … We'll go bankrupt. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"Provided that we raise the money, we'll launch next year.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3","title":"Raise Funds, Launch a Product: Business and Innovation Collocations","objectives":["Students will be able to use business and innovation collocations accurately.","Students will be able to distinguish between words with related meanings (revenue/profit, invent/innovate).","I can say \"launch a product\", \"attract investors\" and \"break even\".","I can explain the difference between revenue and profit."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام متلازمات الأعمال والابتكار بدقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من التمييز بين الكلمات المتقاربة المعنى (revenue/profit وinvent/innovate).","أستطيع أن أقول launch a product وattract investors وbreak even.","أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين revenue وprofit."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"competitor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kəmˈpɛtətɚ/","definition":"a company that sells similar products","arabic":"منافس","example":"The new app has many competitors.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W01"},{"word":"breakthrough","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbreɪkˌθru/","definition":"an important new discovery or development","arabic":"إنجاز / اختراق علمي","example":"The new battery was a major breakthrough.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W02"},{"word":"venture","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɛntʃɚ/","definition":"a new business activity that involves risk","arabic":"مشروع (تجاري)","example":"Opening a restaurant is a risky venture.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W03"},{"word":"bankrupt","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈbæŋkrəpt/","definition":"unable to pay your debts","arabic":"مفلس","example":"The company went bankrupt after three years.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W04"},{"word":"patent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpætənt/","definition":"an official right to be the only one to make or sell an invention","arabic":"براءة اختراع","example":"She applied for a patent for her invention.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W05"},{"word":"cutting-edge","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈkʌtɪŋɛdʒ/","definition":"the most modern and advanced","arabic":"متطوّر / أحدث ما توصّل إليه","example":"The lab uses cutting-edge technology.","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W06"},{"word":"feasible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈfizəbəl/","definition":"possible and practical to do","arabic":"ممكن التنفيذ / عملي","example":"Is your plan financially feasible?","image_id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Business collocations: come up with an idea; develop a prototype; file / apply for a patent; raise funds / capital; attract investors; launch a product; meet demand; gain market share; cut costs; make a profit / a loss; break even (earn exactly what you spend); go bankrupt; run a business.\nRelated words with different meanings: revenue = all the money from sales; profit = the money left after paying all costs. invent = create something completely new; innovate = introduce new ideas or improve something. customer = someone who buys from a shop; client = someone who pays for a professional service; consumer = anyone who buys and uses products.\nAdjectives for innovation: cutting-edge technology, a feasible plan, a risky venture, a major breakthrough, a competitive market, a sustainable business model.\nCollocations/phrases: come up with an idea • develop a prototype • file a patent • raise funds | attract investors • launch a product • meet demand • gain market share | cut costs • make a profit • break even • go bankrupt | a cutting-edge technology • a feasible plan • a risky venture • a major breakthrough","examples":["She came up with the idea while waiting in a long line.","The startup raised five million riyals in its first year.","The company launched its new app in March.","It took the café two years to break even.","The factory couldn't meet demand during Ramadan.","The team filed a patent for its solar cooler.","Our revenue was high, but our profit was low because costs were high.","Developing a prototype helps you test your idea cheaply."],"common_errors":["✗ The company did a profit last year. → ✓ The company made a profit last year. (make a profit.)","✗ We need to collect investors. → ✓ We need to attract investors. (attract investors.)","✗ Our revenue was 10,000 riyals after we paid all our costs. → ✓ Our profit was 10,000 riyals after we paid all our costs. (Money left after costs = profit.)","✗ The company became bankrupt. (informal: went) → ✓ The company went bankrupt. (Fixed phrase: go bankrupt.)"],"arabic":"متلازمات الأعمال: come up with an idea (يخطر بفكرة) وraise funds (يجمع تمويلًا) وattract investors (يجذب مستثمرين) وlaunch a product (يُطلق منتجًا).\nmake a profit (يحقق ربحًا) وbreak even (يغطي تكاليفه دون ربح أو خسارة) وgo bankrupt (يُفلس) وmeet demand (يلبّي الطلب).\nrevenue كل الإيرادات، وprofit ما يتبقى بعد التكاليف. invent يخترع شيئًا جديدًا تمامًا، وinnovate يطوّر ويُدخل أفكارًا جديدة.\nهذه الفروق الدقيقة تُختبر في أسئلة Words in Context في SAT."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Raise Funds, Launch a Product: Business and Innovation Collocations”. Let’s learn business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U2-LS3 | Raise Funds, Launch a Product: Business and Innovation Collocations","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. competitor: a company that sells similar products. breakthrough: an important new discovery or development. venture: a new business activity that involves risk.","on_screen":"competitor | breakthrough | venture","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Business collocations: come up with an idea; develop a prototype; file / apply for a patent; raise funds / capital; attract investors; launch a product; meet demand; gain market share; cut costs; make a profit / a loss; break even (earn exactly what you spend); go bankrupt; run a business. Related words with different meanings: revenue = all the money from sales; profit = the money left after paying all costs. invent = create something completely new; innovate = introduce new ideas or improve something. customer = someone who buys from a shop; client = someone who pays for a professional service; consumer = anyone who buys and uses products.","on_screen":"business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. She came up with the idea while waiting in a long line. The startup raised five million riyals in its first year. The company launched its new app in March.","on_screen":"She came up with the idea while waiting in a long line. / The startup raised five million riyals in its first year. / The company launched its new app in March.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The company did a profit last year.” That’s not right. We say: “The company made a profit last year.” make a profit.","on_screen":"✗ The company did a profit last year.   ✓ The company made a profit last year.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: A company sold 100,000 riyals of products and spent 90,000. What is its revenue? What is its profit? … Think! … Revenue: 100,000. Profit: 10,000. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"launch a product\", \"attract investors\" and \"break even\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can say \"launch a product\", \"attract investors\" and \"break even\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","متلازمات الأعمال: come up with an idea (يخطر بفكرة) وraise funds (يجمع تمويلًا) وattract investors (يجذب مستثمرين) وlaunch a product (يُطلق منتجًا).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The company did a profit last year. — الصحيح: The company made a profit last year.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقول launch a product وattract investors وbreak even."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"She ___ up with the idea while waiting in a long line at the bank.","options":["put","came","went","got"],"answer":"came","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"The startup ___ five million riyals from investors in its first year.","options":["lifted","grew","raised","rose"],"answer":"raised","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"It took the restaurant two years to ___ even.","options":["break","get","make","cut"],"answer":"break","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"All the money a company receives from sales is its ___.","options":["profit","patent","venture","revenue"],"answer":"revenue","explanation":"Distinguish related words (revenue/profit, invent/innovate).","explanation_ar":"الفروق الدقيقة."},{"q":"To protect her invention, she filed a ___.","options":["prototype","profit","patent","venture"],"answer":"patent","explanation":"Business word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Rather than creating an entirely new product, the company chose to ______, improving its existing phone with a longer-lasting battery and a better camera.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["go bankrupt","innovate","invent","break even"],"answer":"innovate","explanation":"Distinguish related words (revenue/profit, invent/innovate).","explanation_ar":"الفروق الدقيقة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the verb to its partner.","items":[{"q":"attract","options":["the high demand during Ramadan","a new product in March","a simple prototype for testing","market share from competitors","costs by using solar energy","investors with a strong pitch"],"answer":"investors with a strong pitch","explanation":"attract → investors with a strong pitch. Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"launch","options":["the high demand during Ramadan","a new product in March","a simple prototype for testing","market share from competitors","costs by using solar energy","investors with a strong pitch"],"answer":"a new product in March","explanation":"launch → a new product in March. Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"meet","options":["the high demand during Ramadan","a new product in March","a simple prototype for testing","market share from competitors","costs by using solar energy","investors with a strong pitch"],"answer":"the high demand during Ramadan","explanation":"meet → the high demand during Ramadan. Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"cut","options":["the high demand during Ramadan","a new product in March","a simple prototype for testing","market share from competitors","costs by using solar energy","investors with a strong pitch"],"answer":"costs by using solar energy","explanation":"cut → costs by using solar energy. Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"gain","options":["the high demand during Ramadan","a new product in March","a simple prototype for testing","market share from competitors","costs by using solar energy","investors with a strong pitch"],"answer":"market share from competitors","explanation":"gain → market share from competitors. Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"develop","options":["the high demand during Ramadan","a new product in March","a simple prototype for testing","market share from competitors","costs by using solar energy","investors with a strong pitch"],"answer":"a simple prototype for testing","explanation":"develop → a simple prototype for testing. Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Talk about business.","items":[{"q":"Describe a product you would like to launch.","options":[],"answer":"I'd like to launch an app that helps students plan their study time.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"How could a small café cut costs?","options":[],"answer":"A small café could cut costs by buying local ingredients.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Why do startups need investors?","options":[],"answer":"Startups need investors to raise funds for development and marketing.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"cutting-edge\".","options":[],"answer":"The hospital uses cutting-edge technology to diagnose diseases.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence explaining \"break even\".","options":[],"answer":"A business breaks even when its revenue equals its costs.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A mini business plan.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences about your business idea using 3 collocations.","options":[],"answer":"We came up with the idea of a date-snack delivery service. We plan to develop a prototype box this month. Then we will try to attract investors.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences about revenue and profit.","options":[],"answer":"We expect revenue of 50,000 riyals in the first year. After costs, we hope to make a small profit.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence about a risk your business faces.","options":[],"answer":"If too many competitors enter the market, we could go bankrupt.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Dominoes","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Deal the dominoes equally in teams of four.","Player 1 places a domino; the next player adds a domino whose word matches the open definition.","If you cannot play, pass.","The first player with no dominoes left wins."],"data":[{"word":"startup","match":"a new, small company, often based on a new idea or technology","arabic":"شركة ناشئة"},{"word":"founder","match":"a person who starts a company or organization","arabic":"مؤسِّس"},{"word":"investor","match":"a person who gives money to a business hoping to make a profit","arabic":"مستثمر"},{"word":"funding","match":"money given for a particular purpose","arabic":"تمويل"},{"word":"innovation","match":"a new idea, method or product","arabic":"ابتكار"},{"word":"prototype","match":"the first model of a new product, used for testing","arabic":"نموذج أولي"},{"word":"revenue","match":"the total money a company receives from sales","arabic":"الإيرادات"},{"word":"profit","match":"money you earn after paying all your costs","arabic":"ربح"},{"word":"competitor","match":"a company that sells similar products","arabic":"منافس"},{"word":"breakthrough","match":"an important new discovery or development","arabic":"إنجاز / اختراق علمي"},{"word":"venture","match":"a new business activity that involves risk","arabic":"مشروع (تجاري)"},{"word":"bankrupt","match":"unable to pay your debts","arabic":"مفلس"},{"word":"patent","match":"an official right to be the only one to make or sell an invention","arabic":"براءة اختراع"},{"word":"cutting-edge","match":"the most modern and advanced","arabic":"متطوّر / أحدث ما توصّل إليه"},{"word":"feasible","match":"possible and practical to do","arabic":"ممكن التنفيذ / عملي"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The new phone was ___ in Riyadh with a big event.","options":["raised","attracted","launched","broken"],"answer":"launched","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"The factory worked day and night to ___ the huge demand.","options":["make","take","do","meet"],"answer":"meet","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"After three bad years, the shop went ___.","options":["venture","profit","bankrupt","broke even"],"answer":"bankrupt","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"Our sales were 200,000 riyals, but after costs, our ___ was only 15,000.","options":["patent","prototype","profit","revenue"],"answer":"profit","explanation":"Distinguish related words (revenue/profit, invent/innovate).","explanation_ar":"الفروق الدقيقة."},{"q":"The team built a ___ to test the design before mass production.","options":["competitor","patent","revenue","prototype"],"answer":"prototype","explanation":"Business word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Two companies sell similar products. They are ___.","options":["competitors","investors","prototypes","founders"],"answer":"competitors","explanation":"Business word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Thomas Edison is famous for ___ many devices, including a practical light bulb.","options":["breaking even","inventing","launching up","innovating"],"answer":"inventing","explanation":"Distinguish related words (revenue/profit, invent/innovate).","explanation_ar":"الفروق الدقيقة."},{"q":"The new solar battery was a major ___ in renewable energy.","options":["venture","bankruptcy","prototype","breakthrough"],"answer":"breakthrough","explanation":"Business word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Before investing, the bank wanted to know whether the plan was financially ______, so the founders showed detailed calculations of their costs and expected sales.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["feasible","cutting-edge","competitive","bankrupt"],"answer":"feasible","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"Opening a restaurant in a city with hundreds of competitors is a risky ______, but the young chef believed his traditional recipes would attract loyal customers.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["prototype","venture","patent","revenue"],"answer":"venture","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"An innovation fair in Riyadh: booths with prototypes (a solar-powered cooler, a smart water bottle, a date-sorting robot); a graph showing revenue and profit lines; young founders talking to investors. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"An innovation fair in Riyadh: booths with prototypes (a solar-powered cooler, a smart water bottle, a date-sorting robot); a graph showing revenue and profit lines; young founders talking to investors"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows competitor (a company that sells similar products); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing competitor: a company that sells similar products"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows breakthrough (an important new discovery or development); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing breakthrough: an important new discovery or development"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows venture (a new business activity that involves risk); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing venture: a new business activity that involves risk"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows bankrupt (unable to pay your debts); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing bankrupt: unable to pay your debts"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows patent (an official right to be the only one to make or sell an invention); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing patent: an official right to be the only one to make or sell an invention"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows cutting-edge (the most modern and advanced); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing cutting-edge: the most modern and advanced"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows feasible (possible and practical to do); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing feasible: possible and practical to do"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Dominoes”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Dominoes"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W01","script":"competitor. competitor. The new app has many competitors.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W02","script":"breakthrough. breakthrough. The new battery was a major breakthrough.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W03","script":"venture. venture. Opening a restaurant is a risky venture.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W04","script":"bankrupt. bankrupt. The company went bankrupt after three years.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W05","script":"patent. patent. She applied for a patent for her invention.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W06","script":"cutting-edge. cutting-edge. The lab uses cutting-edge technology.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-W07","script":"feasible. feasible. Is your plan financially feasible?","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Raise Funds, Launch a Product: Business and Innovation Collocations”. Let’s learn business and innovation collocations (launch a product, raise funds, attract investors, make a profit, break even, go bankrupt, gain market share, meet demand, cut costs, come up with an idea, file a patent); nuances in meaning (profit / revenue; invent / innovate; customer / client / consumer) together. Meet some new words. competitor: a company that sells similar products. breakthrough: an important new discovery or development. venture: a new business activity that involves risk. Business collocations: come up with an idea; develop a prototype; file / apply for a patent; raise funds / capital; attract investors; launch a product; meet demand; gain market share; cut costs; make a profit / a loss; break even (earn exactly what you spend); go bankrupt; run a business. Related words with different meanings: revenue = all the money from sales; profit = the money left after paying all costs. invent = create something completely new; innovate = introduce new ideas or improve something. customer = someone who buys from a shop; client = someone who pays for a professional service; consumer = anyone who buys and uses products. Here are some examples. She came up with the idea while waiting in a long line. The startup raised five million riyals in its first year. The company launched its new app in March. Be careful! Some learners say: “The company did a profit last year.” That’s not right. We say: “The company made a profit last year.” make a profit. Quick check: A company sold 100,000 riyals of products and spent 90,000. What is its revenue? What is its profit? … Think! … Revenue: 100,000. Profit: 10,000. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"launch a product\", \"attract investors\" and \"break even\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS4","title":"However, Therefore, Moreover: Transitions and Logical Relationships (SAT)","objectives":["Students will be able to identify the logical relationship between two sentences and choose the transition that expresses it.","Students will be able to punctuate transitions correctly at the start of a sentence and between two clauses.","I can choose \"however\" for contrast, \"therefore\" for result and \"for example\" for evidence.","I can write \"The idea was simple; however, it worked.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تحديد العلاقة المنطقية بين جملتين واختيار الرابط الذي يعبّر عنها.","سيتمكن الطلاب من ترقيم الروابط بشكل صحيح في أول الجملة وبين جملتين.","أستطيع أن أختار however للتضاد وtherefore للنتيجة وfor example للمثال.","أستطيع أن أكتب The idea was simple; however, it worked."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"startup","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈstɑrˌtʌp/","definition":"a new, small company, often based on a new idea or technology","arabic":"شركة ناشئة","example":"Riyadh has hundreds of technology startups.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"founder","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfaʊndɚ/","definition":"a person who starts a company or organization","arabic":"مؤسِّس","example":"The founder of the company was only twenty-two.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"investor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈvɛstɚ/","definition":"a person who gives money to a business hoping to make a profit","arabic":"مستثمر","example":"The investors were impressed by her business plan.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"funding","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfʌndɪŋ/","definition":"money given for a particular purpose","arabic":"تمويل","example":"The startup received funding from a government program.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"innovation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnəˈveɪʃən/","definition":"a new idea, method or product","arabic":"ابتكار","example":"Innovation is the key to success in technology.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"prototype","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈproʊtəˌtaɪp/","definition":"the first model of a new product, used for testing","arabic":"نموذج أولي","example":"The team built a prototype of the robot in two weeks.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Transitions show the logical relationship between ideas. In SAT Transitions questions, read the sentence before and the sentence after the blank, decide the relationship, and then choose. Addition: moreover, furthermore, in addition, also. Contrast: however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast, by contrast. Concession (admitting a point): admittedly, granted, even so. Cause–effect: therefore, consequently, as a result, thus, hence.\nExample: for example, for instance, specifically. Similarity: similarly, likewise. Sequence / time: first, subsequently, meanwhile, afterward, finally. Clarification: in other words, that is. Emphasis or surprise: in fact, indeed. Replacement: instead, rather.\nPunctuation: at the start of a sentence, put a comma after the transition: However, the plan failed. Between two independent clauses, use a semicolon before and a comma after: The plan was risky; however, it worked. Don't join two sentences with only a comma and a transition: (not: The plan was risky, however, it worked.)\naddition: moreover, furthermore, in addition • contrast: however, nevertheless, in contrast\nresult: therefore, consequently, as a result • example: for example, for instance\nsimilarity: similarly, likewise • sequence: subsequently, meanwhile, finally\nSentence. Transition, … • Clause; transition, clause.","examples":["The app was cheap to build. Moreover, it was easy to use.","The first prototype failed. Nevertheless, the team kept working.","Sales doubled in Ramadan; as a result, the company hired more drivers.","Many startups fail. For example, about half of new businesses close within five years.","Riyadh has become a startup hub. Similarly, Jeddah is attracting young entrepreneurs.","The founders first tested the idea at school. Subsequently, they launched it citywide.","The product isn't new. In fact, similar devices have existed for years.","The company didn't cut prices. Instead, it improved its service."],"common_errors":["✗ The prototype failed. Therefore, the team kept improving it. → ✓ The prototype failed. Nevertheless, the team kept improving it. (Contrast, not result.)","✗ The plan was risky, however, it worked. → ✓ The plan was risky; however, it worked. (Two clauses → semicolon before however.)","✗ Sales were low. For example, the company went bankrupt. → ✓ Sales were low. As a result, the company went bankrupt. (Result, not example.)","✗ The app is fast. In contrast, it is cheap. → ✓ The app is fast. Moreover, it is cheap. (Two positive points → addition.)"],"arabic":"الروابط تُظهر العلاقة المنطقية بين الأفكار. في أسئلة Transitions في SAT اقرأ الجملة قبل الفراغ وبعده وحدّد العلاقة ثم اختر.\nالإضافة: moreover / in addition. التضاد: however / nevertheless / in contrast. النتيجة: therefore / as a result / consequently.\nالمثال: for example / for instance. التشابه: similarly / likewise. التسلسل: subsequently / meanwhile / finally. التوضيح: in other words. التأكيد: in fact.\nالترقيم: فاصلة بعد الرابط في أول الجملة، وفاصلة منقوطة قبله وفاصلة بعده بين جملتين."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “However, Therefore, Moreover: Transitions and Logical Relationships (SAT)”. Let’s learn choosing transitions by logical relationship: addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast), concession (admittedly, even so), cause–effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus), example (for example, for instance), similarity (similarly, likewise), sequence (subsequently, meanwhile, finally), clarification and emphasis (in other words, in fact, indeed); punctuation with transitions together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U2-LS4 | However, Therefore, Moreover: Transitions and Logical Relationships (SAT)","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Transitions show the logical relationship between ideas. In SAT Transitions questions, read the sentence before and the sentence after the blank, decide the relationship, and then choose. Addition: moreover, furthermore, in addition, also. Contrast: however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast, by contrast. Concession (admitting a point): admittedly, granted, even so. Cause–effect: therefore, consequently, as a result, thus, hence.","on_screen":"choosing transitions by logical relationship: addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast), concession (admittedly, even so), cause–effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus), example (for example, for instance), similarity (similarly, likewise), sequence (subsequently, meanwhile, finally), clarification and emphasis (in other words, in fact, indeed); punctuation with transitions","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Transitions show the logical relationship between ideas. In SAT Transitions questions, read the sentence before and the sentence after the blank, decide the relationship, and then choose. Addition: moreover, furthermore, in addition, also. Contrast: however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast, by contrast. Concession (admitting a point): admittedly, granted, even so. Cause–effect: therefore, consequently, as a result, thus, hence. Example: for example, for instance, specifically. Similarity: similarly, likewise. Sequence / time: first, subsequently, meanwhile, afterward, finally. Clarification: in other words, that is. Emphasis or surprise: in fact, indeed. Replacement: instead, rather.","on_screen":"addition: moreover, furthermore, in addition • contrast: however, nevertheless, in contrast • result: therefore, consequently, as a result • example: for example, for instance • similarity: similarly, likewise • sequence: subsequently, meanwhile, finally","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The app was cheap to build. Moreover, it was easy to use. The first prototype failed. Nevertheless, the team kept working. Sales doubled in Ramadan; as a result, the company hired more drivers.","on_screen":"The app was cheap to build. Moreover, it was easy to use. / The first prototype failed. Nevertheless, the team kept working. / Sales doubled in Ramadan; as a result, the company hired more drivers.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The prototype failed. Therefore, the team kept improving it.” That’s not right. We say: “The prototype failed. Nevertheless, the team kept improving it.” Contrast, not result.","on_screen":"✗ The prototype failed. Therefore, the team kept improving it.   ✓ The prototype failed. Nevertheless, the team kept improving it.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"The prototype failed. ___ the team kept working.\" Contrast or result? … Think! … Contrast – *Nevertheless*. Great job! Now you can say: I can choose \"however\" for contrast, \"therefore\" for result and \"for example\" for evidence.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can choose \"however\" for contrast, \"therefore\" for result and \"for example\" for evidence.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: choosing transitions by logical relationship: addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast), concession (admittedly, even so), cause–effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus), example (for example, for instance), similarity (similarly, likewise), sequence (subsequently, meanwhile, finally), clarification and emphasis (in other words, in fact, indeed); punctuation with transitions.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الروابط تُظهر العلاقة المنطقية بين الأفكار. في أسئلة Transitions في SAT اقرأ الجملة قبل الفراغ وبعده وحدّد العلاقة ثم اختر.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The prototype failed. Therefore, the team kept improving it. — الصحيح: The prototype failed. Nevertheless, the team kept improving it.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أختار however للتضاد وtherefore للنتيجة وfor example للمثال."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"How Our Club Became a Company (editing passage)","body":"Last year, our school club designed a solar-powered fan. For example, we only made ten fans for our classrooms. Teachers loved them, therefore other schools asked for them too. Similarly, we had no money to produce more. As a result, a local business offered to help us.","word_count":47,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the best transition.","items":[{"q":"The app was cheap to develop. ___ it was easy for customers to use.","options":["For example,","Moreover,","However,","Instead,"],"answer":"Moreover,","explanation":"Addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition).","explanation_ar":"الإضافة."},{"q":"The first prototype failed. ___ the team kept improving it.","options":["Therefore,","Similarly,","Nevertheless,","For instance,"],"answer":"Nevertheless,","explanation":"Contrast or concession (however, nevertheless, in contrast).","explanation_ar":"التضاد."},{"q":"Sales doubled during Ramadan. ___ the company hired fifty more drivers.","options":["However,","In contrast,","For example,","As a result,"],"answer":"As a result,","explanation":"Cause–effect (therefore, as a result, consequently).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"","options":["The plan was risky; however, it worked.","The plan was risky, however, it worked.","The plan was risky; however it worked.","The plan was risky however, it worked."],"answer":"The plan was risky; however, it worked.","explanation":"Punctuation with transitions.","explanation_ar":"ترقيم الروابط."},{"q":"Riyadh has become a hub for tech startups. ___ Jeddah is attracting more young entrepreneurs every year.","options":["However,","For example,","Therefore,","Similarly,"],"answer":"Similarly,","explanation":"Similarity (similarly, likewise).","explanation_ar":"التشابه."},{"q":"Many people assume that successful founders must have business degrees. ______ several of the region's most successful entrepreneurs studied engineering, design or medicine instead.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Similarly,","As a result,","In fact,","For example,"],"answer":"In fact,","explanation":"Sequence, clarification or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التسلسل والتوضيح والتأكيد."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS4-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Write a suitable transition. Word bank: as a result, However, For example","items":[{"q":"Most new restaurants close within three years. ______, some become local favorites for decades.","options":[],"answer":"However","explanation":"Contrast or concession (however, nevertheless, in contrast).","explanation_ar":"التضاد."},{"q":"The company invested in solar panels; ______, its energy bills fell by 40%.","options":[],"answer":"as a result","explanation":"Cause–effect (therefore, as a result, consequently).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"Many famous products started as small ideas. ______, a popular coffee chain began as a single shop.","options":[],"answer":"For example","explanation":"Example (for example, for instance).","explanation_ar":"المثال."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the transition or its punctuation.","items":[{"q":"The prototype was expensive. For example, investors loved it.","options":[],"answer":"The prototype was expensive. However, investors loved it.","explanation":"For example → However. Contrast or concession (however, nevertheless, in contrast).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: For example → However. التضاد."},{"q":"The app is fast, moreover, it is free.","options":[],"answer":"The app is fast; moreover, it is free.","explanation":", moreover, → ; moreover,. Punctuation with transitions.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , moreover, → ; moreover,. ترقيم الروابط."},{"q":"The factory couldn't meet demand. In contrast, customers waited weeks for their orders.","options":[],"answer":"The factory couldn't meet demand. Consequently, customers waited weeks for their orders.","explanation":"In contrast → Consequently. Cause–effect (therefore, as a result, consequently).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: In contrast → Consequently. النتيجة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the startup story.","items":[{"q":"Last year, our school club designed a solar-powered fan. For example, we only made ten fans for our classrooms.","options":[],"answer":"Last year, our school club designed a solar-powered fan. At first, we only made ten fans for our classrooms.","explanation":"For example → At first. Addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: For example → At first. الإضافة."},{"q":"Teachers loved them, as a result, other schools asked for them too. However, we had no money to produce more. Fortunately, a local business offered to help us.","options":[],"answer":"Teachers loved them; as a result, other schools asked for them too. However, we had no money to produce more. Fortunately, a local business offered to help us.","explanation":", → ;. Addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → ;. الإضافة."},{"q":"Teachers loved them; therefore other schools asked for them too. However, we had no money to produce more. Fortunately, a local business offered to help us.","options":[],"answer":"Teachers loved them; as a result, other schools asked for them too. However, we had no money to produce more. Fortunately, a local business offered to help us.","explanation":"therefore → as a result,. Addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: therefore → as a result,. الإضافة."},{"q":"Teachers loved them; as a result, other schools asked for them too. Similarly, we had no money to produce more. Fortunately, a local business offered to help us.","options":[],"answer":"Teachers loved them; as a result, other schools asked for them too. However, we had no money to produce more. Fortunately, a local business offered to help us.","explanation":"Similarly → However. Addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Similarly → However. الإضافة."},{"q":"Teachers loved them; as a result, other schools asked for them too. However, we had no money to produce more. As a result, a local business offered to help us.","options":[],"answer":"Teachers loved them; as a result, other schools asked for them too. However, we had no money to produce more. Fortunately, a local business offered to help us.","explanation":"As a result → Fortunately. Addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: As a result → Fortunately. الإضافة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write with transitions.","items":[{"q":"Write two sentences about a business idea joined by a contrast transition.","options":[],"answer":"Our snack boxes are healthy. However, they are more expensive than chips.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing transitions by logical relationship: addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast), concession (admittedly, even so), cause–effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus), example (for example, for instance), similarity (similarly, likewise), sequence (subsequently, meanwhile, finally), clarification and emphasis (in other words, in fact, indeed); punctuation with transitions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write two sentences joined by a result transition with a semicolon.","options":[],"answer":"The price was too high; therefore, few students bought the product.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing transitions by logical relationship: addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast), concession (admittedly, even so), cause–effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus), example (for example, for instance), similarity (similarly, likewise), sequence (subsequently, meanwhile, finally), clarification and emphasis (in other words, in fact, indeed); punctuation with transitions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write three sentences about a startup using \"First,\" \"Subsequently,\" and \"Finally,\".","options":[],"answer":"First, the founders tested their idea at school. Subsequently, they opened an online shop. Finally, they launched their product nationwide.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses choosing transitions by logical relationship: addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast), concession (admittedly, even so), cause–effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus), example (for example, for instance), similarity (similarly, likewise), sequence (subsequently, meanwhile, finally), clarification and emphasis (in other words, in fact, indeed); punctuation with transitions correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Speed Spell","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["The teacher says a word and a short sentence with it.","Students write the word in 10 seconds.","Show boards on \"Show me!\". Correct = 1 point.","After 10 words, partners check each other's boards and correct any mistakes."],"data":[{"word":"innovation","clue":"a new idea, method or product"},{"word":"prototype","clue":"the first model of a new product, used for testing"},{"word":"revenue","clue":"the total money a company receives from sales"},{"word":"competitor","clue":"a company that sells similar products"},{"word":"entrepreneur","clue":""},{"word":"feasible","clue":"possible and practical to do"},{"word":"startup","clue":"a new, small company, often based on a new idea or technology"},{"word":"founder","clue":"a person who starts a company or organization"},{"word":"investor","clue":"a person who gives money to a business hoping to make a profit"},{"word":"funding","clue":"money given for a particular purpose"},{"word":"profit","clue":"money you earn after paying all your costs"},{"word":"breakthrough","clue":"an important new discovery or development"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The product is affordable. ___ it comes with a two-year guarantee.","options":["However,","In addition,","In contrast,","Instead,"],"answer":"In addition,","explanation":"Addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition).","explanation_ar":"الإضافة."},{"q":"The startup had very little money. ___ it became one of the fastest-growing companies in the region.","options":["Even so,","Therefore,","Likewise,","For instance,"],"answer":"Even so,","explanation":"Contrast or concession (however, nevertheless, in contrast).","explanation_ar":"التضاد."},{"q":"The company failed to protect its idea with a patent. ___ a competitor copied it within months.","options":["Similarly,","Consequently,","Nevertheless,","For example,"],"answer":"Consequently,","explanation":"Cause–effect (therefore, as a result, consequently).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"Some inventions changed daily life completely. ___ the smartphone transformed how people communicate.","options":["However,","Instead,","For instance,","Nevertheless,"],"answer":"For instance,","explanation":"Example (for example, for instance).","explanation_ar":"المثال."},{"q":"","options":["Sales were low in January; nevertheless, the founders stayed optimistic.","Sales were low in January, nevertheless, the founders stayed optimistic.","Sales were low in January; nevertheless the founders, stayed optimistic.","Sales were low in January nevertheless; the founders stayed optimistic."],"answer":"Sales were low in January; nevertheless, the founders stayed optimistic.","explanation":"Punctuation with transitions.","explanation_ar":"ترقيم الروابط."},{"q":"The company didn't lower its prices. ___ it improved its customer service.","options":["For example,","Similarly,","Therefore,","Instead,"],"answer":"Instead,","explanation":"Sequence, clarification or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التسلسل والتوضيح والتأكيد."},{"q":"The engineers spent two years developing the battery. ______ they spent six months testing it in extreme heat before it went on sale.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["In contrast,","Subsequently,","Nevertheless,","For example,"],"answer":"Subsequently,","explanation":"Sequence, clarification or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التسلسل والتوضيح والتأكيد."},{"q":"Electric cars produce no exhaust fumes. ______ they can help reduce air pollution in crowded cities.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Therefore,","For instance,","In contrast,","However,"],"answer":"Therefore,","explanation":"Cause–effect (therefore, as a result, consequently).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"Traditional banks often reject loan requests from new businesses. ______ some government programs offer startups funding with very low interest rates.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Similarly,","As a result,","For example,","By contrast,"],"answer":"By contrast,","explanation":"Contrast or concession (however, nevertheless, in contrast).","explanation_ar":"التضاد."},{"q":"The founder's first company went bankrupt after two years. ______ she used the lessons she had learned to build a second company that became very successful.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For instance,","Consequently,","Undeterred,","Likewise,"],"answer":"Undeterred,","explanation":"Contrast or concession (however, nevertheless, in contrast).","explanation_ar":"التضاد."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Innovation and Enterprise” that shows choosing transitions by logical relationship: addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast), concession (admittedly, even so), cause–effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus), example (for example, for instance), similarity (similarly, likewise), sequence (subsequently, meanwhile, finally), clarification and emphasis (in other words, in fact, indeed); punctuation with transitions in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Innovation and Enterprise” that shows choosing transitions by logical relationship: addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast), concession (admittedly, even so), cause–effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus), example (for example, for instance), similarity (similarly, likewise), sequence (subsequently, meanwhile, finally), clarification and emphasis (in other words, in fact, indeed); punctuation with transitions in action"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Speed Spell”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Speed Spell"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “However, Therefore, Moreover: Transitions and Logical Relationships (SAT)”. Let’s learn choosing transitions by logical relationship: addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast), concession (admittedly, even so), cause–effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus), example (for example, for instance), similarity (similarly, likewise), sequence (subsequently, meanwhile, finally), clarification and emphasis (in other words, in fact, indeed); punctuation with transitions together. Transitions show the logical relationship between ideas. In SAT Transitions questions, read the sentence before and the sentence after the blank, decide the relationship, and then choose. Addition: moreover, furthermore, in addition, also. Contrast: however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast, by contrast. Concession (admitting a point): admittedly, granted, even so. Cause–effect: therefore, consequently, as a result, thus, hence. Transitions show the logical relationship between ideas. In SAT Transitions questions, read the sentence before and the sentence after the blank, decide the relationship, and then choose. Addition: moreover, furthermore, in addition, also. Contrast: however, nevertheless, on the other hand, in contrast, by contrast. Concession (admitting a point): admittedly, granted, even so. Cause–effect: therefore, consequently, as a result, thus, hence. Example: for example, for instance, specifically. Similarity: similarly, likewise. Sequence / time: first, subsequently, meanwhile, afterward, finally. Clarification: in other words, that is. Emphasis or surprise: in fact, indeed. Replacement: instead, rather. Here are some examples. The app was cheap to build. Moreover, it was easy to use. The first prototype failed. Nevertheless, the team kept working. Sales doubled in Ramadan; as a result, the company hired more drivers. Be careful! Some learners say: “The prototype failed. Therefore, the team kept improving it.” That’s not right. We say: “The prototype failed. Nevertheless, the team kept improving it.” Contrast, not result. Quick check: \"The prototype failed. ___ the team kept working.\" Contrast or result? … Think! … Contrast – *Nevertheless*. Great job! Now you can say: I can choose \"however\" for contrast, \"therefore\" for result and \"for example\" for evidence.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS5","title":"Pitching an Idea: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to present a startup pitch using conditionals, business collocations and clear transitions.","Students will be able to write a short pitch and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can pitch a business idea to a group of \"investors\".","I can write a persuasive pitch with conditions and transitions."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تقديم عرض لفكرة مشروع ناشئ باستخدام الشروط ومتلازمات الأعمال والروابط الواضحة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة عرض قصير والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أعرض فكرة مشروع على مجموعة من «المستثمرين».","أستطيع أن أكتب عرضًا مقنعًا بالشروط والروابط."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"startup","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈstɑrˌtʌp/","definition":"a new, small company, often based on a new idea or technology","arabic":"شركة ناشئة","example":"Riyadh has hundreds of technology startups.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"founder","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfaʊndɚ/","definition":"a person who starts a company or organization","arabic":"مؤسِّس","example":"The founder of the company was only twenty-two.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"investor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈvɛstɚ/","definition":"a person who gives money to a business hoping to make a profit","arabic":"مستثمر","example":"The investors were impressed by her business plan.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"funding","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfʌndɪŋ/","definition":"money given for a particular purpose","arabic":"تمويل","example":"The startup received funding from a government program.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"innovation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnəˈveɪʃən/","definition":"a new idea, method or product","arabic":"ابتكار","example":"Innovation is the key to success in technology.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"prototype","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈproʊtəˌtaɪp/","definition":"the first model of a new product, used for testing","arabic":"نموذج أولي","example":"The team built a prototype of the robot in two weeks.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"revenue","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɛvəˌnu/","definition":"the total money a company receives from sales","arabic":"الإيرادات","example":"The company's revenue doubled last year.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"profit","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈprɑfət/","definition":"money you earn after paying all your costs","arabic":"ربح","example":"The café made a profit in its second year.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"competitor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kəmˈpɛtətɚ/","definition":"a company that sells similar products","arabic":"منافس","example":"The new app has many competitors.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"breakthrough","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbreɪkˌθru/","definition":"an important new discovery or development","arabic":"إنجاز / اختراق علمي","example":"The new battery was a major breakthrough.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"venture","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɛntʃɚ/","definition":"a new business activity that involves risk","arabic":"مشروع (تجاري)","example":"Opening a restaurant is a risky venture.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"bankrupt","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈbæŋkrəpt/","definition":"unable to pay your debts","arabic":"مفلس","example":"The company went bankrupt after three years.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"patent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpætənt/","definition":"an official right to be the only one to make or sell an invention","arabic":"براءة اختراع","example":"She applied for a patent for her invention.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"cutting-edge","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈkʌtɪŋɛdʒ/","definition":"the most modern and advanced","arabic":"متطوّر / أحدث ما توصّل إليه","example":"The lab uses cutting-edge technology.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"feasible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈfizəbəl/","definition":"possible and practical to do","arabic":"ممكن التنفيذ / عملي","example":"Is your plan financially feasible?","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 2: mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business and innovation collocations, and transitions that show logical relationships.\nA strong pitch explains the problem, presents the solution, gives evidence, explains what you need (funding, partners) and what will happen under certain conditions. Transitions guide investors through your argument: however (a challenge), therefore (a decision), moreover (an extra benefit), for example (evidence).\n✔ If we hadn't…, we wouldn't be… • If we were…, we would have…\n✔ unless • provided that • as long as • in case • otherwise\n✔ come up with, develop a prototype, raise funds, launch a product, make a profit\n✔ ; however, • Therefore, • Moreover, • For example,","examples":["If we hadn't tested our idea, we wouldn't know what students want.","As long as the price stays low, demand will be high.","We'll keep extra stock in case demand grows quickly.","We plan to break even in the first year and make a profit in the second.","Moreover, the bottle is made from recycled plastic.","Our product is new; however, similar apps already exist.","Sales will rise in summer; therefore, we will launch in May.","For example, our survey showed that most students forget to drink water."],"common_errors":["✗ If we didn't do a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now. (the survey was in the past) → ✓ If we hadn't done a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now. (Past condition → past perfect.)","✗ Unless we don't keep the price low, students won't buy it. → ✓ Unless we keep the price low, students won't buy it. (No double negative.)","✗ We built a prototype, however, we need funding. → ✓ We built a prototype; however, we need funding. (Semicolon before however.)"],"arabic":"نراجع الشروط المختلطة وبدائل if ومتلازمات الأعمال والروابط المنطقية.\nالعرض القوي يشرح المشكلة والحل ويقدّم الأدلة ويوضّح ما تحتاجه وما سيحدث بشروط معيّنة.\nالروابط ترشد المستثمرين عبر حجتك: however للتحدي، therefore للقرار، moreover لفائدة إضافية، for example للدليل."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Pitching an Idea: Language in Use”. Let’s learn mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U2-LS5 | Pitching an Idea: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Investor panel: groups prepare a 90-second pitch for a product that solves a school problem. Include the problem, the solution, a mixed conditional, a condition with provided that / unless, two business collocations and three transitions. The \"investors\" ask one question with \"Suppose…\" and decide whether to invest.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 2: mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business and innovation collocations, and transitions that show logical relationships. A strong pitch explains the problem, presents the solution, gives evidence, explains what you need (funding, partners) and what will happen under certain conditions. Transitions guide investors through your argument: however (a challenge), therefore (a decision), moreover (an extra benefit), for example (evidence).","on_screen":"✔ If we hadn't…, we wouldn't be… • If we were…, we would have… • ✔ unless • provided that • as long as • in case • otherwise • ✔ come up with, develop a prototype, raise funds, launch a product, make a profit","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. If we hadn't tested our idea, we wouldn't know what students want. As long as the price stays low, demand will be high. We'll keep extra stock in case demand grows quickly.","on_screen":"If we hadn't tested our idea, we wouldn't know what students want. / As long as the price stays low, demand will be high. / We'll keep extra stock in case demand grows quickly.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “If we didn't do a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now. (the survey was in the past).” That’s not right. We say: “If we hadn't done a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now.” Past condition → past perfect.","on_screen":"✗ If we didn't do a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now. (the survey was in the past)   ✓ If we hadn't done a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “Unless we don't keep the price low, students won't buy it.”? … Think! … It should be “Unless we keep the price low, students won't buy it.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can pitch a business idea to a group of \"investors\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can pitch a business idea to a group of \"investors\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع الشروط المختلطة وبدائل if ومتلازمات الأعمال والروابط المنطقية.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: If we didn't do a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now. (the survey was in the past) — الصحيح: If we hadn't done a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أعرض فكرة مشروع على مجموعة من «المستثمرين»."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Our Pitch: HydroMate – Noura's Team","body":"Our team's idea is a smart water bottle that reminds students to drink water during the school day.\nWe came up with the idea after noticing that many of our classmates felt tired in the afternoon.\nIf we hadn't done a survey in our school, we wouldn't know that 70% of students drink less than one liter a day.\nWe have already built a simple prototype; however, we need funding to improve the design.\nProvided that we raise 20,000 riyals, we will be able to launch the product next year.\nUnless we keep the price low, students won't buy it; therefore, we plan to sell it for under 50 riyals.\nIf our school hadn't supported us, we wouldn't be presenting this pitch today.","word_count":123,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the pitch. True or False?","items":[{"q":"The team's product is a smart water bottle.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Our Pitch: HydroMate – Noura's Team”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"They came up with the idea after a teacher complained.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Our Pitch: HydroMate – Noura's Team”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Their survey showed that most students drink less than one liter a day.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Our Pitch: HydroMate – Noura's Team”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"They have not built anything yet.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Our Pitch: HydroMate – Noura's Team”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"They need 20,000 riyals to launch the product.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Our Pitch: HydroMate – Noura's Team”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"They plan to sell the bottle for more than 100 riyals.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Our Pitch: HydroMate – Noura's Team”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"If the team hadn't done a survey, they ___ about the problem now.","options":["won't know","wouldn't have known","wouldn't know","didn't know"],"answer":"wouldn't know","explanation":"Mixed conditional or alternative to if.","explanation_ar":"الشرط."},{"q":"___ they raise 20,000 riyals, they will launch the product next year.","options":["Provided that","In case","Otherwise","Unless"],"answer":"Provided that","explanation":"Mixed conditional or alternative to if.","explanation_ar":"الشرط."},{"q":"The team came ___ the idea after noticing that classmates felt tired.","options":["out of","over to","down on","up with"],"answer":"up with","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"","options":["They built a prototype, however, they need funding.","They built a prototype; however, they need funding.","They built a prototype; however they, need funding.","They built a prototype however they need funding."],"answer":"They built a prototype; however, they need funding.","explanation":"Punctuation with transitions.","explanation_ar":"ترقيم الروابط."},{"q":"___ they keep the price low, students won't buy the bottle.","options":["As long as","Provided that","Unless","In case"],"answer":"Unless","explanation":"Mixed conditional or alternative to if.","explanation_ar":"الشرط."},{"q":"The bottle reminds students to drink water every hour. ______ it records how much they drink and shares the data with an app.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["In addition,","In contrast,","However,","Instead,"],"answer":"In addition,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Prepare your pitch.","items":[{"q":"The problem you want to solve: ___","options":[],"answer":"Students waste a lot of paper in our school.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Your product or service: ___","options":[],"answer":"An app that lets students submit homework digitally.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A mixed conditional sentence: ___","options":[],"answer":"If we hadn't counted the paper we used, we wouldn't realize how big the problem is.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A condition with \"provided that\" or \"as long as\": ___","options":[],"answer":"Provided that teachers agree, we can test the app next term.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Two business collocations: ___","options":[],"answer":"develop a prototype, attract investors","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your pitch.","items":[{"q":"Plan: problem, solution, evidence, what you need, conditions, transitions.","options":[],"answer":"long lines at the canteen; pre-order app; survey: 15-minute wait; need 10,000 riyals; provided that the school agrees; however / therefore / moreover","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your pitch in 8–9 sentences.","options":[],"answer":"Every day, students wait up to fifteen minutes in line at our school canteen. Our team came up with a simple solution: a pre-order app. If we hadn't timed the lines ourselves, we wouldn't know how serious the problem is. We have already developed a prototype; however, we need 10,000 riyals to complete it. Provided that the school agrees, we will test the app next term. Moreover, the canteen will be able to plan its food better and waste less. Unless students find the app easy, they won't use it; therefore, we will keep the design simple. We believe this app can make lunchtime faster for everyone.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: mixed conditional? unless without a double negative? transitions punctuated correctly? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"We have a prototype, however, we need money\" to \"We have a prototype; however, we need money.\"","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spin the Review Wheel","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["A player spins the wheel and lands on a word.","Say what the word means and use it in a sentence with mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","Teammates check: meaning (1 point), grammar (1 point), pronunciation (1 point).","Highest total after 6 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"startup","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"founder","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"investor","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"funding","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"innovation","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"prototype","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"revenue","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"profit","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"competitor","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"breakthrough","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"venture","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"bankrupt","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"patent","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"cutting-edge","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"feasible","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"If I were a better speaker, I ___ our team's pitch last week.","options":["would present","would have presented","had presented","will present"],"answer":"would have presented","explanation":"Mixed conditional or alternative to if.","explanation_ar":"الشرط."},{"q":"You can use our logo ___ you mention our company's name.","options":["in case","unless","as long as","otherwise"],"answer":"as long as","explanation":"Mixed conditional or alternative to if.","explanation_ar":"الشرط."},{"q":"The startup needs to ___ more investors before it can grow.","options":["draw in on","collect","attract","gain up"],"answer":"attract","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"It took the company eighteen months to ___.","options":["make even","go even","cut even","break even"],"answer":"break even","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"","options":["Sales were slow; therefore the company, cut its prices.","Sales were slow therefore the company cut its prices.","Sales were slow, therefore, the company cut its prices.","Sales were slow; therefore, the company cut its prices."],"answer":"Sales were slow; therefore, the company cut its prices.","explanation":"Punctuation with transitions.","explanation_ar":"ترقيم الروابط."},{"q":"The new app was praised for its design. ______ many users complained that it drained their phone batteries.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Likewise,","For example,","Therefore,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"The founders wanted their company to help the environment. ______ they chose suppliers that use only recycled packaging.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Nevertheless,","In contrast,","Accordingly,","For example,"],"answer":"Accordingly,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Saudi Vision 2030 encourages entrepreneurship.\n• Government programs offer training and funding to startups.\n• The number of startups in Saudi Arabia has grown in recent years.\n• Many startups focus on technology and e-commerce.\n\nThe student wants to explain why the number of Saudi startups has grown. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Many Saudi startups focus on technology and e-commerce.","Supported by government programs that offer training and funding, more and more Saudi entrepreneurs are launching startups.","Saudi Vision 2030 is a national plan.","The number of startups in Saudi Arabia has changed."],"answer":"Supported by government programs that offer training and funding, more and more Saudi entrepreneurs are launching startups.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• A prototype is an early model of a product.\n• Building a prototype is usually cheap.\n• Prototypes help teams find problems early.\n• Fixing problems after launch can be very expensive.\n\nThe student wants to explain why startups should build prototypes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Because fixing problems after launch can be very expensive, startups should build cheap prototypes that help them find problems early.","Problems can be found in many products.","Building a prototype is usually cheap.","A prototype is an early model of a product."],"answer":"Because fixing problems after launch can be very expensive, startups should build cheap prototypes that help them find problems early.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best sentence for a pitch:","options":["If we didn't test our idea, we wouldn't be so confident today, moreover our survey shows demand.","If we hadn't tested our idea with real students, we wouldn't be so confident today; moreover, our survey shows strong demand.","Unless we didn't test our idea, we wouldn't be confident; however, our survey shows demand.","If we hadn't tested our idea, we wouldn't have been confident today; moreover our survey, shows demand."],"answer":"If we hadn't tested our idea with real students, we wouldn't be so confident today; moreover, our survey shows strong demand.","explanation":"Mixed conditional or alternative to if.","explanation_ar":"الشرط."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A school \"Shark Tank\"-style event (without logos): a team of students presenting a smart water bottle prototype to a panel of teachers acting as investors; a slide showing \"70% of students drink less than 1 liter a day\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A school \"Shark Tank\"-style event (without logos): a team of students presenting a smart water bottle prototype to a panel of teachers acting as investors; a slide showing \"70% of students drink less than 1 liter a day\""},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Innovation and Enterprise”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Innovation and Enterprise”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L09-U2-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spin the Review Wheel”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spin the Review Wheel"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS5-T1","script":"Our team's idea is a smart water bottle that reminds students to drink water during the school day. We came up with the idea after noticing that many of our classmates felt tired in the afternoon. If we hadn't done a survey in our school, we wouldn't know that 70% of students drink less than one liter a day. We have already built a simple prototype; however, we need funding to improve the design. Provided that we raise 20,000 riyals, we will be able to launch the product next year. Unless we keep the price low, students won't buy it; therefore, we plan to sell it for under 50 riyals. If our school hadn't supported us, we wouldn't be presenting this pitch today.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U2-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Pitching an Idea: Language in Use”. Let’s learn mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business collocations, logical transitions; SAT rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Investor panel: groups prepare a 90-second pitch for a product that solves a school problem. Include the problem, the solution, a mixed conditional, a condition with provided that / unless, two business collocations and three transitions. The \"investors\" ask one question with \"Suppose…\" and decide whether to invest. Today you use all of Unit 2: mixed conditionals, alternatives to if, business and innovation collocations, and transitions that show logical relationships. A strong pitch explains the problem, presents the solution, gives evidence, explains what you need (funding, partners) and what will happen under certain conditions. Transitions guide investors through your argument: however (a challenge), therefore (a decision), moreover (an extra benefit), for example (evidence). Here are some examples. If we hadn't tested our idea, we wouldn't know what students want. As long as the price stays low, demand will be high. We'll keep extra stock in case demand grows quickly. Be careful! Some learners say: “If we didn't do a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now. (the survey was in the past).” That’s not right. We say: “If we hadn't done a survey, we wouldn't know the problem now.” Past condition → past perfect. Quick check: what is wrong with “Unless we don't keep the price low, students won't buy it.”? … Think! … It should be “Unless we keep the price low, students won't buy it.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can pitch a business idea to a group of \"investors\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"If I had learned to code as a child, I ___ my own app now.","options":["would have had","would have","will have","had"],"answer":"would have","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"If she ___ so shy, she would have asked the investor a question at the conference.","options":["isn't","won't be","weren't","hadn't been being"],"answer":"weren't","explanation":"Present condition → past result.","explanation_ar":"شرط حاضر ← نتيجة ماضية."},{"q":"If the company had hired more engineers, its product ___ so many bugs today.","options":["won't have","wouldn't have had","wouldn't have","hadn't had"],"answer":"wouldn't have","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"If we had more time, we ___ a better prototype.","options":["will build","had built","would have built","would build"],"answer":"would build","explanation":"Second conditional (present + present).","explanation_ar":"الشرط الثاني."},{"q":"If the team had tested the design, the launch ___ so badly last month.","options":["won't go","didn't go","wouldn't go","wouldn't have gone"],"answer":"wouldn't have gone","explanation":"Third conditional (past + past).","explanation_ar":"الشرط الثالث."},{"q":"If the founders had not chosen to focus on a single product in 2018, the company ______ as successful as it is today.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["might not be","may not been","might not been","might not have"],"answer":"might not be","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."},{"q":"___ you have a clear plan, the bank won't lend you money.","options":["Unless","Provided","In case","As long as"],"answer":"Unless","explanation":"unless = if… not.","explanation_ar":"unless."},{"q":"You can join the project ___ you share the work fairly.","options":["unless","provided that","in case","otherwise"],"answer":"provided that","explanation":"as long as / provided that = only if.","explanation_ar":"بشرط أن."},{"q":"Bring a printed copy of your slides ___ the projector doesn't work.","options":["unless","in case","as long as","provided"],"answer":"in case","explanation":"in case = as a precaution.","explanation_ar":"احتياطًا."},{"q":"Back up your files every day; ___, you could lose months of work.","options":["otherwise","provided","unless","even if"],"answer":"otherwise","explanation":"otherwise = if not.","explanation_ar":"وإلا."},{"q":"___ the competition is difficult, we'll do our best.","options":["In case of","Even if","Unless","Provided that"],"answer":"Even if","explanation":"even if = it doesn't matter whether.","explanation_ar":"حتى لو."},{"q":"The team spent months trying to ___ investors for its new app.","options":["gain up","attract","draw on","collect"],"answer":"attract","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"The company's ___ was high, but its profit was low because its costs were huge.","options":["prototype","patent","revenue","venture"],"answer":"revenue","explanation":"Distinguish related words (revenue/profit, invent/innovate).","explanation_ar":"الفروق الدقيقة."},{"q":"The inventor protected her design by filing a ___.","options":["patent","profit","competitor","revenue"],"answer":"patent","explanation":"What really happened?","explanation_ar":"ماذا حدث فعلًا؟"},{"q":"The company introduced a ______ water-recycling system that uses sensors and artificial intelligence, the most advanced technology of its kind in the region.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["cutting-edge","traditional","feasible","bankrupt"],"answer":"cutting-edge","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The new product sold so fast that the factory couldn't ___ demand.","options":["take","do","make","meet"],"answer":"meet","explanation":"Business collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة الأعمال."},{"q":"The company was cheap to start. ___ it grew quickly because customers loved its service.","options":["For example,","Instead,","Moreover,","However,"],"answer":"Moreover,","explanation":"Addition (moreover, furthermore, in addition).","explanation_ar":"الإضافة."},{"q":"The prototype failed three tests. ___ the engineers refused to give up.","options":["Similarly,","For instance,","Therefore,","Nevertheless,"],"answer":"Nevertheless,","explanation":"Contrast or concession (however, nevertheless, in contrast).","explanation_ar":"التضاد."},{"q":"","options":["The market was competitive however the startup succeeded.","The market was competitive; however the startup, succeeded.","The market was competitive, however, the startup succeeded.","The market was competitive; however, the startup succeeded."],"answer":"The market was competitive; however, the startup succeeded.","explanation":"Punctuation with transitions.","explanation_ar":"ترقيم الروابط."},{"q":"Many startups struggle to raise money in their first year. ______ a recent study found that only one in ten receives funding from professional investors.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["In contrast,","Indeed,","Instead,","Nevertheless,"],"answer":"Indeed,","explanation":"Sequence, clarification or emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التسلسل والتوضيح والتأكيد."},{"q":"The café switched from plastic to paper cups. ______ its customers produced far less plastic waste.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Similarly,","As a result,","For example,"],"answer":"As a result,","explanation":"Cause–effect (therefore, as a result, consequently).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Abbas ibn Firnas lived in Córdoba in the ninth century.\n• He was an inventor and scientist.\n• He built a pair of wings from wood and feathers.\n• He tried to fly from a hill and glided for a short distance.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize that Ibn Firnas was an early pioneer of flight. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Abbas ibn Firnas lived in Córdoba in the ninth century.","Córdoba was home to many scientists.","Abbas ibn Firnas was an inventor and a scientist.","More than a thousand years ago, the Córdoban inventor Abbas ibn Firnas built wings of wood and feathers and glided a short distance—an early attempt at human flight."],"answer":"More than a thousand years ago, the Córdoban inventor Abbas ibn Firnas built wings of wood and feathers and glided a short distance—an early attempt at human flight.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"unless we don't find investors, we will close the company, however we will try.\"","options":["unless we find investors, we will close the company; however, we will keep trying.","Unless we don't find investors, we will close the company; however, we will try.","Unless we find investors, we will close the company; however, we will keep trying.","Unless we find investors, we will close the company, however, we will try."],"answer":"Unless we find investors, we will close the company; however, we will keep trying.","explanation":"unless = if… not.","explanation_ar":"unless."},{"q":"\"If I hadn't missed that flight, I wouldn't be married to my wife today.\" What does this tell us?","options":["Missing the flight led to meeting his wife.","He will miss a flight tomorrow.","He didn't miss the flight.","He isn't married."],"answer":"Missing the flight led to meeting his wife.","explanation":"Past condition → present result.","explanation_ar":"شرط ماضٍ ← نتيجة حاضرة."}],"theme_ar":"الابتكار وريادة الأعمال","essential_question_ar":"ماذا يتطلّب تحويل فكرة إلى مشروع ناجح؟","can_do":"I can use mixed conditionals and alternatives to if (unless, provided that, as long as, otherwise, suppose, in case), talk about business and innovation with precise collocations, and choose transitions that show the right logical relationship.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أستخدم الجمل الشرطية المختلطة وبدائل if (unless وprovided that وas long as وotherwise وsuppose وin case)، وأتحدث عن الأعمال والابتكار بمتلازمات دقيقة، وأختار الروابط التي تُظهر العلاقة المنطقية الصحيحة."});
